        ":question/id": "3250f349-fbfe-56f8-96e2-52cb212003b3",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-102142",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/7d/7dad50d17444096c60b129131104209b8b7371d546f80d7b0c838baec1df8b4d.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x+6?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5),B(1,2)$ and $C(2,-4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (5) & =-5(0)+6 \\\\ 5 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (2) & =-5(1)+6 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (-4) & =-5(2)+6 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line."
      },
