        ":question/problem": "![](images/1a/1aa0849898f763eedd84d050a7bd7c24bbb10c8f89c81e3f533622252330814e.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=3x+4.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,-5)$, $B(1,5)$ and $C(2,10).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,-5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+4 \\\\ (-5) & =3(-3)+4 \\\\ -5 & =-5\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =7\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,10)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+4 \\\\ (10) & =3(2)+4 \\\\ 10 & =10\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."
