        ":question/problem": "![](images/1f/1ff37e7ea63a62de2a1ccbf59c53225b739be305f9e87f7aceca7735ba210bbd.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-3x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,8),B(0,-2)$ and $C(2,-6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,8)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\ (8) & =-3(-2)+0 \\\\ 8 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,-2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\ (-2) & =-3(0)+0 \\\\ -2 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\ (-6) & =-3(2)+0 \\\\ -6 & =-6\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line."
